# terraform

Deploys {{ project_name }} to AWS Lambda via the shared
[`lambda-web-app`](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules/tree/main/modules/lambda-web-app)
module{% if needs_datastore %},
[`dynamodb-single-table`](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules/tree/main/modules/dynamodb-single-table){% endif %},
with state in S3 via
[`s3-bucket`](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules/tree/main/modules/s3-bucket).

Every `source =` in here is a tag-pinned `terraform-modules` reference, and the
`terraform` CI job fails if one isn't — add resources to that repo and release a
tag, not to this directory. See
[STANDARDS.md](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/project-template/blob/main/STANDARDS.md)'s
Hosting section.

## Before first `terraform apply`

1. Create the state bucket. Once per project, not once per deploy:
   ```bash
   cd bootstrap && terraform init && terraform apply && cd ..
   ```
   `bootstrap/` is a separate root config whose only job is that bucket, and its
   own state is a local, gitignored file — a config that creates the state bucket
   can't keep its state in it. **`terraform init` up here fails with
   `NoSuchBucket` until this is done**, having changed nothing.
2. Build the Lambda artifact:
   ```bash
   cd ../backend && npm run build:lambda
   ```
   This runs `backend/scripts/build-lambda.js`, which stages `backend/src`,
   production-only `node_modules`,{% if needs_ui %} `frontend/`'s static files (into
   `dist-lambda/web`, matching the `WEB_DIST` that `main.tf` sets),{% endif %} and the
   `run.sh` the Lambda Web Adapter execs. Re-run it after **any** {% if needs_ui %}backend or
   frontend{% else %}backend{% endif %} change — `terraform apply` zips this directory, it does not
   build it.
3. Create every SSM parameter referenced in `main.tf`'s `ssm_secret_env_vars`:
   ```bash
   aws ssm put-parameter --name "/{{ project_slug }}/..." --type SecureString --value "..."
   ```
4. `terraform init && terraform apply`

`terraform apply` needs `python3` on PATH — the module zips the artifact with
a helper script rather than `archive_file`, so `run.sh` keeps its executable
bit even when the zip is built on Windows.

To check syntax without AWS access, `terraform init -backend=false && terraform
validate` — a plain `init` now contacts S3, and fails without credentials. That's
what CI runs, in both this directory and `bootstrap/`.

## State

State is at `terraform.tfstate` in the bucket `bootstrap/` created, named by
`backend.tf`. Locking is `use_lockfile = true` — S3-native, so there's no
DynamoDB lock table to provision or forget. Backend blocks can't interpolate, so
the bucket name and region are literals in both `backend.tf` and
`bootstrap/main.tf`, rendered from the same scaffold answers; change one and you
change both.

Versioning is on, with the module's 90-day noncurrent-version expiry. That's
recovery from a bad apply last week, not an archive of last year — and it's what
keeps a bucket written on every apply from quietly outgrowing the 5GB free
allowance.

Losing `bootstrap/terraform.tfstate` breaks nothing that's deployed; the bucket
and everything in it are untouched. What it costs you is management of the
bucket: re-applying `bootstrap/` then fails with `BucketAlreadyOwnedByYou`.
Either `terraform import` the module's resources back (`aws_s3_bucket.this`,
`aws_s3_bucket_versioning.this`, and the SSE, ownership, public-access-block,
`aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration.this[0]` and bucket-policy resources
under `module.tf_state`, all keyed on the bucket name), or leave the bucket
unmanaged — it costs nothing until you need to change it.

## Tearing it down

Order matters and is not symmetric with setup:

1. `terraform destroy` in **this** directory first.
2. The state bucket deliberately survives — `force_destroy = false`, so
   `destroy` in `bootstrap/` refuses a non-empty bucket. To actually remove it,
   set `force_destroy = true` in `bootstrap/main.tf`, `apply`, then `destroy`.
   Emptying it by hand is fiddlier than it looks: `aws s3 rm --recursive` leaves
   noncurrent versions and delete markers behind, so the naive retry fails the
   same way.

**Destroying the bucket while this directory's state is still in it strands
everything that state described** — the Lambda, the Function URL, the log group
and any table keep existing, and nothing is left that knows about them. Nothing
fails loudly when you do it in that order, which is the reason for this section.
{% if needs_ui %}

## Adding static assets

Nothing to do — `build-lambda.js` copies all of `frontend/` into
`dist-lambda/web` except a fixed set of tooling files (`node_modules`,
`package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `test/`, `vitest.config.js`). A new asset
ships automatically. The flip side is that anything you leave in `frontend/`
is published, so don't park scratch files or secrets there.
{% endif %}
